# text = That well-bred attendant knows how to interpret the most obscure diagnosis of all mental diseases that can afflict her mistress ; she knows when the ivory complexion is bought and paid for -- when the pearly teeth are foreign substances fashioned by the dentist -- when the glossy plaits are the relics of the dead , rather than the property of the living ; and she knows other and more sacred secrets than these ; she knows when the sweet smile is more false than Madame Levison's enamel , and far less enduring -- when the words that issue from between gates of borrowed pearl are more disguised and painted than the lips which help to shape them -- when the lovely fairy of the ball-room re-enters the dressing-room after the night's long revelry , and throws aside her voluminous burnous and her faded bouquet , and drops her mask , and like another Cinderella loses the glass-slipper , by whose glitter she has been distinguished , and falls back into her rags and dirt , the lady's maid is by to see the transformation .
